%I A399136 #6 Aug 25 2026 00:50:24
%S A399136 11,15,19,21,24,26,28,29,32,34,36,37,39
%N A399136 a(n) is the smallest number of cells in a polycube that has a cavity consisting of exactly n cells.
%C A399136 A cavity of a polycube is a finite face-connected component of its complement in the cubic lattice (empty space sealed off from the outside); cf. A396101.
%C A399136 This is the three-dimensional analog of A283056 (smallest polyomino admitting a hole of size n).
%C A399136 a(n) = min over connected n-cell void shapes R of ( |shell(R)| + kappa(R) ), where shell(R) is the set of lattice cells face-adjacent to R (all forced to be occupied), and kappa(R) is the least number of additional cells needed to join the shell into a single polycube - a lattice node-weighted Steiner-tree quantity.
%C A399136 The minimizing void shapes are compact: for example a(8) = 29 is attained by the 2 X 2 X 2 cube void, and a(4) = 21 by a 2 X 2 X 1 square void.
%C A399136 Computed by two independently-written programs, which agree for all n <= 13; the underlying enumeration reproduces the fixed-polycube counts A001931 at every size.
%e A399136 a(1) = 11: to seal a single empty cell, every one of its 6 face-neighbors must be occupied; those 6 cells are mutually non-adjacent, so at least 5 further cells are needed to bind them into one connected polycube, giving 6 + 5 = 11.
%Y A399136 Cf. A283056 (two-dimensional analog), A396101 (fixed polycubes with a cavity), A355966, A357083, A001931.
%K A399136 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A399136 1,1
